# Managed by xstage — rendered into the arm64 catalyst confdir by
# 'xstage arm64 setup' (confdir_sync_overlay). Never edit the copy inside the
# releng clone: it is regenerated, and releng is an upstream checkout that
# xstage clones once and never pulls, so a hand edit there would exist on one
# machine only.
#
# Why the chroot may not build stable gcc: every compile inside it is shipped
# by distcc to the HOST's crossdev gcc (distcc_hosts in catalyst-arm64.conf),
# and 'xarm distcc chroot-setup' refuses a major mismatch outright — mixing
# majors means ICEs or silent miscompilation. Without this file the chroot
# resolves STABLE arm64 gcc (15.3.0 today) while the cross toolchain is
# gcc-@GCC_PREV@, and the stage3 it produces would ship a libstdc++ older than
# everything later cross-built against it.
#
# Upper-bounded at the next major on purpose — the same rule xarm applies to
# the sysroot's own xarm-toolchain file. A bare '~@SUBARCH@' would also expose
# whatever gcc-<next> snapshot the tree happens to carry that week.
<sys-devel/gcc-@GCC_NEXT@ ~@SUBARCH@
